WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.651
This paper, A View From Somewhere,

00:00:02.731 --> 00:00:07.703
published at ICLR 2023 by researchers at Sony AI
and the University of Tokyo,

00:00:07.783 --> 00:00:10.984
rethinks how we represent human faces in datasets.

00:00:11.064 --> 00:00:15.774
Instead of relying on problematic demographic
labels like race or gender,

00:00:15.854 --> 00:00:21.679
the authors collect over six hundred thousand human
judgments of face similarity and learn

00:00:21.759 --> 00:00:25.550
a continuous embedding space aligned with human
perception.

00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:29.356
The result is a face representation that is interpretable,

00:00:29.436 --> 00:00:33.949
that avoids invasive categorical labels, and that
captures the smooth,

00:00:34.029 --> 00:00:37.098
continuous nature of human phenotypic diversity,

00:00:37.178 --> 00:00:41.560
opening a new path for measuring dataset diversity
more responsibly.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.313
Evaluating the diversity of face datasets almost
always relies on categorical demographic labels such as race,

00:00:46.393 --> 00:00:50.767
gender, or age.

00:00:50.847 --> 00:00:55.220
But these labels are frequently unavailable,
legally restricted to collect,

00:00:55.300 --> 00:00:59.673
and prone to bias when they are inferred by
annotators rather than self-reported.

00:00:59.753 --> 00:01:04.127
Worse,

00:01:04.207 --> 00:01:08.580
categorical labels flatten the continuous nature of
human appearance: skin tone becomes simply light or dark,

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:13.033
and two faces sharing the same label can still look
strikingly different.

00:01:13.113 --> 00:01:17.487
Rigid taxonomies also erase multi-ethnic people,
shift meaning across cultures,

00:01:17.567 --> 00:01:21.940
and can cause real psychological harm when someone
is mislabeled.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:28.770
The authors argue that face similarity is
fundamentally continuous and depends on who is doing the judging.

00:01:28.850 --> 00:01:33.286
If we could learn a representation directly aligned
with human perception,

00:01:33.366 --> 00:01:38.596
we could sidestep problematic semantic labels
entirely while still measuring diversity.

00:01:38.676 --> 00:01:45.797
Existing psychological-embedding methods fall
short: they either cannot embed new faces beyond the fixed training set,

00:01:45.877 --> 00:01:48.056
or they pool every judgment together,

00:01:48.136 --> 00:01:53.060
throwing away the meaningful differences within and
between individual annotators.

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:57.322
Their answer is A View From Somewhere, or AVFS.

00:01:57.403 --> 00:02:01.285
They collect a dataset of six hundred thirty-eight
thousand triplet similarity judgments

00:02:01.365 --> 00:02:05.248
over nearly five thousand faces,

00:02:05.328 --> 00:02:09.210
and crucially,

00:02:09.290 --> 00:02:13.173
each judgment records the identity and
self-reported demographics of the annotator who made it.

00:02:13.253 --> 00:02:17.135
On top of this data they build a conditional
learning framework that produces a continuous,

00:02:17.215 --> 00:02:21.098
low-dimensional, and human-interpretable embedding space,

00:02:21.178 --> 00:02:25.060
along with per-annotator masks that reveal how
different people weigh different visual dimensions.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:29.754
At the core is a conditional convolutional network,
a ResNet eighteen,

00:02:29.834 --> 00:02:34.274
that maps each face to a
one-hundred-twenty-eight-dimensional embedding.

00:02:34.354 --> 00:02:38.166
Training uses odd-one-out judgments: given a
triplet of faces,

00:02:38.246 --> 00:02:40.739
an annotator picks the least similar one.

00:02:40.819 --> 00:02:45.573
Each annotator is assigned a learnable gating mask,
passed through a sigmoid,

00:02:45.653 --> 00:02:50.092
that scales the importance of every embedding
dimension for that person.

00:02:50.172 --> 00:02:54.110
Similarity between two faces is the dot product of
their masked,

00:02:54.190 --> 00:02:55.428
rectified embeddings,

00:02:55.508 --> 00:03:01.642
and the model predicts the odd-one-out probability
directly from these three pairwise similarities.

00:03:01.722 --> 00:03:06.727
A sparsity and non-negativity penalty keeps the
dimensions few and interpretable,

00:03:06.807 --> 00:03:09.740
leaving only about twenty-two active dimensions.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:15.658
The AVFS dataset comprises six hundred thirty-eight
thousand quality-controlled triplet

00:03:15.738 --> 00:03:20.696
judgments over four thousand nine hundred
twenty-one near-frontal face images.

00:03:20.776 --> 00:03:27.154
Every single judgment carries the annotator's
identifier and self-identified demographic attributes.

00:03:27.234 --> 00:03:31.351
The authors evaluate on held-out judgments over the
same stimuli,

00:03:31.431 --> 00:03:34.903
on eighty thousand judgments over entirely novel faces,

00:03:34.983 --> 00:03:40.780
and against attribute-recognition baselines trained
on CelebA and FairFace semantic labels.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:45.343
Across both the same-stimuli and the novel-stimuli
evaluations,

00:03:45.422 --> 00:03:49.605
the AVFS models predict human similarity judgments
more accurately than baselines trained on semantic labels,

00:03:49.685 --> 00:03:53.867
and they better capture the uncertainty in human
judgments as measured by Spearman correlation.

00:03:53.947 --> 00:03:58.130
Importantly,

00:03:58.210 --> 00:04:02.392
the annotator-specific masks generalize even to
triplets made entirely of novel,

00:04:02.472 --> 00:04:06.655
previously unseen faces,

00:04:06.735 --> 00:04:10.917
which shows the embedding space is far more closely
aligned with the human mental representation

00:04:10.997 --> 00:04:15.180
of faces than spaces learned from rigid categorical
labels.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:17.999
To test whether annotators really matter,

00:04:18.079 --> 00:04:23.653
the authors randomly shuffle which annotator mask
is attached to each of the eighty thousand

00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:26.971
judgments and recompute accuracy a hundred times over.

00:04:27.051 --> 00:04:31.887
Accuracy drops from about sixty-two percent down to
roughly fifty-three percent,

00:04:31.967 --> 00:04:35.451
proving that annotators are genuinely not interchangeable.

00:04:35.531 --> 00:04:40.797
A dimension-elimination analysis further shows that
only six to thirteen dimensions are

00:04:40.877 --> 00:04:43.870
needed to recover most of the predictive accuracy,

00:04:43.950 --> 00:04:49.585
while fifteen to twenty-two dimensions are required
to explain the full similarity structure,

00:04:49.665 --> 00:04:52.780
clear evidence that similarity is context-dependent.

00:04:53.160 --> 00:04:55.733
The headline numbers tell the whole story.

00:04:55.813 --> 00:05:03.124
Six hundred thirty-eight thousand human judgments
gathered over four thousand nine hundred twenty-one distinct faces.

00:05:03.204 --> 00:05:09.630
Twenty-two interpretable embedding dimensions
retained at nearly sixty-two percent validation accuracy.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:12.410
And when annotator identities are scrambled,

00:05:12.490 --> 00:05:17.463
accuracy falls from sixty-one point seven down to
fifty-two point eight percent,

00:05:17.543 --> 00:05:21.380
all achieved without ever training on a single
semantic label.

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:27.005
The key takeaway is that learning face
representations from human similarity judgments,

00:05:27.085 --> 00:05:31.167
instead of demographic labels, produces a
continuous, interpretable,

00:05:31.247 --> 00:05:37.900
and perception-aligned embedding space that
supports diversity analysis without invasive categorical labeling.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:43.593
And because the framework only requires keeping
track of which annotator made which judgment,

00:05:43.673 --> 00:05:48.857
the same conditional approach can extend naturally
to almost any machine-learning task

00:05:48.937 --> 00:05:51.060
built on subjective human decisions.
